---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import WidgetShowcase from '../components/WidgetShowcase.astro';
import BackgroundShowcase from '../components/BackgroundShowcase.astro';
import ThemeGallery from '../components/ThemeGallery.astro';
import ProductMedia from '../components/ProductMedia.astro';
import { themeByName } from '../lib/catalog';
import { deckMedia } from '../lib/media';
import '../styles/home.css';

const repo = 'https://github.com/UrDeck/urdeck';
const heroAlt = themeByName('glass')!.alt;
---
<Base title="UrDeck" description="A lightweight widget dashboard for a secondary or case display on Windows. No account, no telemetry, free software." bleed>

  <section class="hero" id="top">
    <div aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="aurora aurora-a"></div>
      <div class="aurora aurora-b"></div>
      <div class="hero-fade"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="chip"><i aria-hidden="true"></i>Free software for Windows 10 and 11</p>
        <h1>A small dashboard for <span>the screen beside your screen.</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          UrDeck puts clocks, system readings, weather and shortcuts on a secondary or case display. It is a native Windows
          app drawn on the GPU, and it redraws only what changed, so a page with nothing moving costs almost nothing.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={repo}>Download: coming soon</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={repo}>
            <svg aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M12 .5C5.65.5.5 5.65.5 12c0 5.08 3.29 9.39 7.86 10.91.58.1.79-.25.79-.56v-2c-3.2.7-3.87-1.37-3.87-1.37-.52-1.33-1.28-1.69-1.28-1.69-1.04-.71.08-.7.08-.7 1.15.08 1.76 1.19 1.76 1.19 1.03 1.76 2.69 1.25 3.35.96.1-.74.4-1.25.73-1.54-2.55-.29-5.24-1.28-5.24-5.69 0-1.26.45-2.28 1.19-3.09-.12-.29-.52-1.46.11-3.05 0 0 .97-.31 3.17 1.18a11 11 0 0 1 5.78 0c2.2-1.49 3.17-1.18 3.17-1.18.63 1.59.23 2.76.11 3.05.74.81 1.19 1.83 1.19 3.09 0 4.42-2.69 5.39-5.25 5.68.41.36.78 1.06.78 2.14v3.17c0 .31.21.67.8.56A11.5 11.5 0 0 0 23.5 12C23.5 5.65 18.35.5 12 .5z"/></svg>
            Source on GitHub
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          There is no download yet: a release will come once its files can be signed, so Windows can tell they are mine. Until
          then the button leads to the repository, where UrDeck can be built from source.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="device">
        <div class="device-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="bezel">
          <div class="bezel-screen">
            <ProductMedia slot="deck" media={deckMedia} alt={heroAlt} widths={[300, 600, 720]} sizes="19rem" loading="eager" autoplay />
            <script is:inline>window.urdeckTheme && urdeckTheme.swap(document.querySelector('[data-slot="deck"]'));</script>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>The <span data-theme-name>Glass</span> theme, drawn by UrDeck itself · 1100 × 3840</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why it exists</p>
    <p class="statement">
      I wanted the small screen in my case to show things <span>without a program behind it that I cannot trust or see into.</span>
    </p>

    <div class="bento">
      <article class="card wide">
        <div>
          <p class="num">01</p>
          <h3>Private.</h3>
          <p class="prose">No account, no telemetry. UrDeck makes two kinds of network request, both for something you put on the page. Nothing else leaves your machine, and a local icon file avoids even that.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="well ledger">
          <div class="d head">outbound requests</div>
          <div><span class="a">&rarr;</span><span class="k">a forecast</span><span class="d">for a place you typed into a weather card</span></div>
          <div><span class="a">&rarr;</span><span class="k">an icon</span><span class="d">for a web address you gave a shortcut</span></div>
          <div><span class="d">&times;</span><span class="k">everything else</span><span class="d">none</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card narrow">
        <div>
          <p class="num">02</p>
          <h3>Light.</h3>
          <p>A native app that draws each widget on the GPU and redraws only what changed. A moving background is optional and has a setting to make it cheaper.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stats-row">
            <div class="stat"><b>0<small>%</small></b><span>CPU, idle</span></div>
            <div class="stat"><b>0<small>%</small></b><span>GPU, idle</span></div>
            <div class="stat"><b>101<small> MB</small></b><span>private memory</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="foot">Measured on the WinUI 3 host with one Clock widget on screen.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card narrow">
        <div>
          <p class="num">03</p>
          <h3>Yours.</h3>
          <p>One JSON file, themes you can write in a few lines, backgrounds written as small shaders. Widgets are plain C# plugins that load and reload while UrDeck runs.</p>
        </div>
        <pre><code><span class="c">// urdeck-config.json</span>
{ <span class="k">"typeId"</span>: <span class="s">"urdeck.widgets.clock"</span>,
  <span class="k">"width"</span>: 4, <span class="k">"height"</span>: 2,
  <span class="k">"format"</span>: <span class="s">"24h"</span>,
  <span class="k">"style"</span>: <span class="s">"flap"</span> }</code></pre>
        <p class="small">A config editor, so you will not have to open the file, and a gallery for sharing widgets, backgrounds and themes are planned, not built.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card wide">
        <div>
          <p class="num">04</p>
          <h3>Open.</h3>
          <p class="prose">The source is on GitHub under the GPL. The plugin SDK is MIT, so a widget can use any licence.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="licences">
          <div class="well"><b>GPL</b><span>the application</span></div>
          <div class="well"><b class="accent">MIT</b><span>the plugin SDK</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ThemeGallery />

  <section class="section" id="widgets">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Widgets</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Built-in widgets, and more on the way.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each widget draws itself on the GPU and sits on a grid of square cells, and some change shape with the room they are
        given. Pick one to look closer. Others can be added as plain C# plugins.
      </p>
    </div>
    <WidgetShowcase />
  </section>

  <BackgroundShowcase />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="closing">
      <div class="aurora aurora-1" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="aurora aurora-2" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="closing-copy">
        <h2>Build it from source today. A signed download follows.</h2>
        <p>A release will come once its files can be signed, so Windows can tell they are real. Until then the repository has everything you need.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={repo}>Source on GitHub</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/">Configure it</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="links">
      <a class="link-card" href="/docs/"><b>Docs <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i></b><span>The config file, pages, the dock, each widget, themes and backgrounds.</span></a>
      <a class="link-card" href="/tested-on/"><b>Tested on <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i></b><span>The hardware it has run on, and how to report yours.</span></a>
      <a class="link-card" href={repo}><b>Repository <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i></b><span>Source, roadmap and issues.</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
